 command role under King Philip VI and later served Kings John II and Charles V, embodying the martial traditions of the French aristocracy. His career spanned a critical juncture when the French monarchy struggled against English incursions and internal factionalism, positioning him at the heart of royal efforts to consolidate power and defend the realm.

Montmorency distinguished himself early through military service, notably participating in campaigns in Brittany and the sieges of cities like Calais. Although he resigned his Marshal's baton in 1347, he remained a trusted advisor and military leader, commanding various royal contingents. He was captured by the English in 1356, prior to the catastrophic Battle of Poitiers, a testament to the continuous skirmishes and shifting fortunes of the war. His release and subsequent service to Charles V, particularly during the reconquest phases led by Bertrand du Guesclin, underscore his enduring loyalty and experience in a period marked by shifting allegiances and constant warfare.

His legacy is primarily tied to the continued ascent of the Montmorency family, whose prestige he helped cement through his high offices and military service. While not a figure who single-handedly altered the course of the Hundred Years' War, his consistent presence in the royal council and on the battlefield exemplified the resilience and adaptive strategies of the French nobility in the face of unprecedented national crisis. He contributed to the administrative and military structures that would eventually allow France to reclaim much of its lost territory and lay the groundwork for a stronger Valois monarchy."}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-02T09:20:11Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#36988894650", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1307"}, {"title": "Military conflicts and strategic necessities", "type": "Idea", "year": "1307"}], "newValue": ["The ongoing Hundred Years' War necessitated experienced military commanders and loyal nobles for the French crown.", "His lineage as a member of the powerful and ancient House of Montmorency provided him with significant social standing and opportunities for advancement.", "The feudal structure of 14th-century France relied heavily on the military service and political support of its leading aristocrats."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-02T09:20:11Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#36988894650", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Determined military outcomes shaping geopolitical boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "1381-09-11"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1381-09-11"}], "newValue": ["His military and political service helped to sustain the Valois monarchy during its most precarious period.", "He contributed to the enduring prestige and infl